## Classification

- **Document type:** Conceptual / systems-design paper, peer-reviewed (ACM ICAIF)
- **Primary audience:** AI/ML model-risk practitioners, regulators, technologists
- **AI explicit:** Yes — entirely focused on AI/ML model governance distinct from traditional model risk management

## 3–5 Reusable Findings

1. Diagnoses a fundamental mismatch: traditional MRM frameworks (built around OCC/CRD IV/CRR-style transparent models with clear parameter–output dependencies) cannot adequately govern AI/ML models whose "intrinsically encoded data representations" lack explicit, auditable dependencies (feeds S6).
2. Documents that current AI model governance relies on "complex review flows" and "heavily manual steps," creating effectiveness, cost, complexity, and speed problems that worsen as model complexity grows — direct evidence for the [[Governance_Debt]] construct applied to AI/ML specifically (feeds S6).
3. The COVID-19 pandemic served as a real-world "natural stress test": AI models in financial services experienced serious accuracy/reliability degradation, and firms struggled to recalibrate/retrain at speed — a citable empirical instance of model-risk crystallisation (feeds S6).
4. Proposes a system-level "self-regulation" framework integrating continuous monitoring, management, and mitigation — explicitly NOT full automation, but an augmentation of governance via system design, positioned as a third lever alongside policy and process (feeds S4/S6).
5. Frames OCC's risk-tiered periodic review cadence (quarterly/semi-annual/annual by model-risk classification) as the baseline regulatory expectation that AI governance frameworks (incl. RBI's draft MRM circular, [[07_Institutions/Corpus/RBI/RBI_3]]) must adapt for AI-specific risk characteristics (feeds S6).

## Consulting / Teaching Reuse

- The "traditional model transparency vs. AI opacity" framing is a strong slide-level explainer for why legacy 3LoD/MRM frameworks (cf. F006 SR 11-7, F003 IIA Three Lines Model) need AI-specific adaptation — directly supports AEGIS_OS's "runtime enforcement" pitch.
- The COVID stress-test example is a ready-made case study for board-level risk-committee briefings on AI model fragility.

